Date: Tue, 23 Nov 93 22:15:38 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: GP for cryptanalysis, algorithm design, etc.

Young and Loud:
>         In a lot of ways, this is identical to what a chess-playing program
> does.  Or anything else which searches a huge tree.  
> 
>         What I haven't seen (I'd like a reference if it's around, though)
> is a GA/P routine which does this kind of search. 

GA/GP is far more general purpose than tree searching.  Given a
Turing-complete terminal and function set (eg Chaitin's "pure
Lisp" subset, Teller's state machine terminal+function set
for GP, etc.) GP searches an arbitary but exponentially 
decreasing subset of the entire space of finite, soon-halting
Turing machines, including Turing machines that 
implement tree search!   GA can also be set up to do this,
by encoding the TM as a binary string.

So if, for example, any algorithm exists to 
factor in polynomial time (thought to be quite improbable since
with much effort no human has found a polynomial solution to this
or a large class of related problems), GP with a fitness function favoring
fast and correct solutions to factoring test cases might well
find it.  With the caveat that some regions of Turing space may be 
"deceptive", or unreasonably spiky; this is an open theoretical 
question.  Also, searching the space of long-running cryptanalytic 
algorithms might take an unreasonable amount of time, even with 100's 
of Crays, Connection Machines, etc. to play with.  In general, any 
space that contains clues or partial solutions, eg DES known plaintext 
attacks, most algorithm design problems, etc. is
susceptible to GA/GP.  (although performance may be inferior 
to special-purpose algorithms like those used in differential 
cryptanalysis).

Of course, one could dump the TM-completeness in favor of a
search tree (eg decision tree) and search it probabilistically.
This would probably perform worse than special-purpose tree search
for special-purpose tree search problems.  

For more info, see Koza, _Genetic Programming_ and
the excellent genetic-programming@cs.stanford.edu mailing 
list.

Incidentally, the best known algorithms for factoring are,
like GA/GP, probabilistic, although they finish with much higher 
confidence than GA/GP (which is notorious for its unpredictability
in time-til-solution, a necessary comcommitant to its generality).

