Date: Tue, 23 Nov 93 22:50:38 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: GP algorithm discovery rate

Craig Presson:
> ...the NSA with its billions of flops, possibly trillions
> 10 years from now, could employ more GP's than they employ mathematicians
> for far less money.

The good news is the performance of GP is proportional to the speed
of the fitness cases, and that fast algorithms with fast tests (eg finding
primes) can be tested with orders of magnitude fewer cycles than the 
typically slow algorithms for cryptanalysis (factoring, 
detection of patterns in a pseudorandom sequence, etc.).
If the problems have similar testing/verification times, and
assuming similar editing distance between improvements in the 
search spaces for the fast and slow algorithms, improvements in fast
algorithms will be discovered by GP far faster than improvements in
slow algorithms.   (Alas this fast/slow dichotomy 
doesn't correspond exactly to the cryptography/cryptanalysis
dichotomy, since some fast cryptographic algorithms, like PRNG's, 
require slow algorithms to properly test.)

This interesting phenomenon may even apply to human researchers,
if as is often observed human induction proceeds by an
analogous process of recombination of ideas, trial-and-error,
etc.

The main interests of current GP researchers are complex problems like
protein classification, robotics, and the like.  But as a little
side-effort, Koza has already used GP to generate a PRNG 
with higher entropy than any human-designed algorithm. 

