circulation magazines. Most startups want to stay away from this. This
requires superb distribution and efficient manufacturing. Typically, low
margins are the norm and a "win" is done by execution in manufacturing,
marketing and distribution. Often, this becomes a commodity strategy  The
costs of getting a distribution system can be a killer, and advertising
becomes frightfully expensive and can be a rough and tumble play. I
wouldn't touch it, but be my guest. If you have a unique way to position
what you're doing, then it might be a better play, but at that point begins
to approach a niche play. 


NICHE MARKET
Part of the overall market is there for the taking. Seeking a specialized
protected segment becomes the objective (e.g., Apple for many years with
desktop publishing, Denon stereo equipment, Kurzweil synthesizers, network
management utilities, etc). Each of these takes a segement of the overall
market and pursues it but delivering some unique benefit to the market
(this may be psychological...Rolex doesn't necessarily keep time better
than some Timex). This is a less costly approach, but viscious competition
can exist for a tiny niche. Good news: you know your competitors; bad news:
they know you and might hire away key people. I believe this is a good
strategy because it allows marketing efforts to be highly refined and
targeted, thus allowing nimbleness.

"Adobe"/OEM
This can be a dream because it removes most of the marketing costs, but
puts a high emphasis on personal relationships, reliability, and leaves you
a slave to the vagaries of another company's management decision-making.
Think about Adobe. They enjoyed wonderful success because of the power of
their IP (intellectual property) of postscript. For every Apple Laserwriter
that sold, Adobe got a cut. An OEM strategy can be a bit different
(OEM=original equipment manufacturer) where you make X and Y includes it or
just puts his/her label on it. In the "Adobe" strategy, the IP drives the
deal, and as long as its unique and not priced out of the market, a company
either plays ball or goes home. This is a dream and if you can do this, do
it. The OEM strategy has as its customers others who will take on the
marketing and distribution costs (of your boards, embedded software, ...)
but you have to keep them happy, show your quality meets spec, deliver on
time, aren't priced too high, blah blah blah. Friends of mine around
Silicon Valley who've pursued this for years live in big houses up in the
hills and swear by it (Silicon Valley is the land of the $100,000 house
with the $1,000,000 mortgage). It's also problematic and I'm aware of many,
many small startups that begin with this strategy and got eaten and whose
owners now work for other companies having lost theirs.


ALLIANCE/THREAT
Sometimes, the purpose of a startup is to be bought ASAP. Depending on the
the existence of intellectual property, a company may see an opportunity to
develop a widget or fenetcam that makes another company's product work much
better, but the developers don't want the hassle of going to market. As
soon as they have demoware or prototypes (S.Brand: "Demo or Die") they look
for buyers. This can be for an alliance partner ("we'll make this, you use
it exclusively and pay us oodles") or as a threat ("we're making this and
it'll obsolete your razamatazz by the end of next quarter"). Either of
these approaches can get another company to buy you out, or at least buy
out the rights (maybe exclusive) to your product and either (1) absorb the
product and/or company, or (2) bury it. If this has been a strategy from
day one, the owners walk away with much cash and go do something else. This
is not a strategy for kids. It can be rough. It also can happen at
bankruptcy with most unfavorable terms. Successful ones tend to head from
this from the moment they're out of the gate.

....

Admittedly, these are very, very cursory treatments and my MBA friends
would hose me with vitrol for being so cavalier with them (afterall,
internal rates of return are important, convergence of technologies count,
contribution margins are reasons to invest or not to, many refinements are
important...), but the purpose here was to point toward on key business
fact -- be every mindful of who comprises the target market of the company,
and what strategy is to be used to reach them. This was an attempt to take
a VERY simplistic cut at a most difficult problem, but in the process to
offer some food for thought.

Comments invited.

