Date: Wed, 24 Nov 1993 11:55:35 +0000 (GMT)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: Comments on NSA

rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu writes:

>   I don't believe the NSA can crack RSA, otherwise they wouldn't be
>so worried about it. On the other hand, the NSA does have one of the
>largest supercomputing sites in the world so it's plausible that they could
>brute force smaller keys, DES, etc, if a massive need occured. I'm far
>more afraid of the NSA's _listening_ capability right now. They are probably
>state of the art signal analysis. They don't need to crack PGP if they can
>sit in a van outside your house and read your keystrokes. Imagine
>if the NSA had voice recogonition software running on international phone
>calls. They could literally _GREP_ people's phone conversations, millions
>of them, without employing a standing army of spooks.

>   I wouldn't be surprised of lots of interstate calls are intercepted
>this way too.

You're not being very realistic. I'd expect ALL interstate, and
probably ALL local calls to be recorded. I'd expect SOME calls -- from
people flagged as suspicious -- to be keyword monitored. And anyone
who shows up as a centre for crypto traffic is going to AUTOMATICALLY
attract attention (as, if nothing else, a possible foreign agent or
terrorist or drug trafficker).

Here in the UK I did a back-of-envelope calculation a few months ago.
There are 30 Million phones here. Assume an average of 1 hour/day
usage. Allow 2kbits/sec bandwidth for an uncompressed voice call. Each
phone therefore generates 7.6 mbits/day of data, or <1 Mbyte. Thats
30,000 gigabytes. A lot? Hell, no. There are c. 1000 digital exchanges
in the UK. Allowing the use of 4.5 Gbyte DAT drives at each exchange,
you can spool off EVERY phone call in the country by having each
exchange load a tape, record everything, and send it in to Secret
Police HQ on a daily basis.

That's without compression, of course ...

For identifying subversive networks you rely on traffic analysis.  The
network is a nondirected cyclic graph with 30 million nodes.  Each node
has a call list associated with it, consisting of a destination node
(the path to the destination is irrelevant unless in-band-signalling is
possible, and even then it's only relevant if the caller's a phone
phreak), a start time, and a duration.  Certain nodes are already
flagged as 'interesting', i.e.  surveillance in progress.  These nodes
are listened to.  If a call from node A (under surveillance) goes to
node B (clean), and a call from node B _then_ goes to node C (under
surveillance), you can assume a transitive dependency between A->B->C
and therefore include B in your search network.

I believe that Solzhenitsyn wrote in the 70's that once more than 20%
of the population has a telephone, the secret police can't monitor
calls effectively any more. (Hence the Moscow phone system :) This
assertion should now be judged obsolete, as digital switches make it
far too easy to trace subversive networks and offline spooling of all
calls makes it possible to compile a _history_ of a network.

The phone system is compromised. Face it. And so, very probably, is
the Internet.


-- Charlie

