Date: Wed, 24 Nov 1993 07:55:51 -0600 (CST)
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Comments on NSA

Charlie Stross:

> Here in the UK I did a back-of-envelope calculation a few months ago.
> There are 30 Million phones here. Assume an average of 1 hour/day
> usage. Allow 2kbits/sec bandwidth for an uncompressed voice call.

> That's without compression, of course ...

Interesting thing to calculate.  I'd be really interested in the
actual average use (though 1 hour seems approximately reasonable).
Unfortunately, the 2kbits/sec uncompressed figure is off by at
least an order of magnitude.  With 8-bit samples (about the minimum
that could be used), and a 4K sampling rate (not really enough, but
being generous), that's 32kbits/sec.  There are ways to compress
voice so that it's more or less understandable at 5kbits/sec (used
by the military), but those would require vast (unrealistic) amounts
of computing power for your telephone company to have installed to do
the compression.  Realistically, 10kbps is a minimum figure with feasible
compression.

Now, even if the 2kpbs figure was right, you dropped another decimal
point:  30,000 gigabytes (1MB * 30M phones) is right.  1000 digital
exchanges -> 30 GB / exchange / day == ~10 4MB DAT tapes.  Given
the actual uncompressed rate of at least 20kbps, now we're talking
about 100 DAT tapes per day per exchange.  Still possible, but logistically
rather more difficult; per month, the phone company (or the government,
or whoever) would be buying three million DAT tapes.  If such is happening
it shouldn't be very difficult to verify.

